        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Working With Lists",
            Slug = "everywhere-lists",
            Summary = "Search, sort, filter, hide and reorder columns, and export — every list in Forge works the same way",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 6,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/parts","/customers","/sales-orders","/purchase-orders","/invoices","/vendors","/employees"]""",
            Tags = """["everywhere","lists","filters","columns","export"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Working With Lists\n\nParts, customers, orders, invoices, employees, vendors — almost every page in Forge opens with a list. They all share one table, so once you know how one list works you know them all. Nothing you do to a list changes the underlying records; you are only changing how you look at them.\n\n### Search\n\nMost list pages have a search box above the table. Type part of a name, number or description and the list narrows as you type. You do not need the whole value — `brack` finds *Bracket, L-shaped*.\n\n### Sort\n\nClick a column heading to sort by it. Click again to flip the direction; a third click clears the sort. To sort by more than one column at once, hold **Shift** while you click the second heading — the list sorts by the first column, then by the second within it. The small arrow in the heading shows which columns are sorting and which way.\n\n### The column menu\n\nEvery column heading has a three-dot button on its right edge (right-clicking the heading opens the same menu). It gathers everything you can do to that column:\n\n- **Sort Ascending / Sort Descending / Clear Sort**\n- **Filter...** — opens the filter for that column\n- **Clear Filter** and **Clear All Filters** — shown once a filter is active\n- **Hide Column**\n- **Reset Width** — shown after you have dragged a column edge\n\nWhen a column has a filter on it, the three-dot button is tinted and shows a dot so you can tell at a glance why rows are missing.\n\n### Filters\n\nWhat the filter asks for depends on the kind of column:\n\n- **Text columns** — choose a match rule (Contains, Equals, Starts with, Ends with, Does not contain, Does not equal) and type the value.\n- **Number columns** — a Min and a Max. Leave either one blank for an open-ended range.\n- **Date columns** — a From and a To date.\n- **Choice columns** such as Status — tick the values you want to keep.\n\nClick **Apply** to use the filter or **Clear** to remove it. Filters on several columns stack: a row has to pass all of them to show.\n\n### Columns\n\nThe gear button at the top-right corner of the table opens the column manager. Tick or untick a column to show or hide it, and drag the handle on the left of a name to reorder. **Reset** puts everything back the way the page shipped. You can also drag the right edge of any heading to make a column wider or narrower.\n\n### Export\n\nNext to the gear are two download buttons: **Export CSV** and **Export Excel**. Both export what you are looking at — the rows that pass your filters, in your sort order, with the columns you have showing. Hide the columns you do not want before you export and you get a clean file with no cleanup.\n\n### Forge remembers\n\nYour sort, filters, column order, hidden columns, column widths and page size are saved per list, for you. Leave the Parts page and come back next week and it looks the way you left it. Nobody else's view changes when you change yours. If a list looks wrong and you cannot work out why, check for tinted three-dot buttons (active filters) and use **Clear All Filters** or the column manager's **Reset**.\n\n### Paging\n\nLong lists are shown a page at a time, with the page controls at the bottom of the table. The count next to them tells you how many rows match right now, which is a quick way to see whether a filter did what you meant.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
