        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Bulk Actions on Jobs",
            Slug = "everywhere-bulk-actions",
            Summary = "Select several jobs at once on the Board or the Backlog and move, assign, prioritize, archive or restore them in one step",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 3,
            AppRoutes = """["/kanban","/backlog"]""",
            Tags = """["everywhere","bulk","jobs","kanban","backlog"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Bulk Actions on Jobs\n\nWhen the same change applies to a dozen jobs, you do not have to open them one at a time. The Job Board and the Backlog let you select several jobs and act on all of them together.\n\n### Selecting more than one\n\nOn the **Job Board**, hold **Ctrl** (or **Cmd** on a Mac) and click each card you want. Selected cards are highlighted and a bar appears showing how many are selected. On the **Backlog**, tick the checkbox at the left of each row, or tick the box in the heading to select every row on the page. **Clear selection** on the bar deselects everything without doing anything else.\n\n### What you can do\n\nWith jobs selected on the Job Board, the bar offers:\n\n- **Move** — send every selected job to a stage you pick. Jobs that are not allowed to make that move are left where they are, and Forge tells you how many could not move and why.\n- **Assign** — hand every selected job to one person, or choose *Unassigned* to clear the assignee.\n- **Priority** — set the same priority on all of them.\n- **Archive** — take them off the board. Forge asks you to confirm and shows the count. Archived jobs are not deleted; they move to the Backlog's archived view.\n\nOn the **Backlog**, selecting archived jobs lets you **Unarchive** them back onto the board in one go.\n\n### Nothing is silent\n\nEach bulk action reports what happened: *Moved 8 jobs to Assembly*, *Assigned 5 jobs to Maria*, *3 jobs could not be moved*. If some jobs were skipped, the ones that could change did change, and the ones that could not are listed so you can deal with them individually. Read the message before you move on.\n\n### Good habits\n\n- Filter or search first, then select, so you are only acting on the jobs you can see and mean to change.\n- Use bulk **Assign** at the start of a shift and bulk **Move** at the end of one; it keeps the board honest without a lot of dragging.\n- Archive in batches once a week rather than letting finished jobs pile up.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Attaching Files to Records",
            Slug = "everywhere-attachments",
            Summary = "Drawings, photos, certs and customer paperwork live on the record they belong to — how to add them, view them and keep them tidy",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 4,
            AppRoutes = """["/parts","/sales-orders","/quotes","/employees"]""",
            Tags = """["everywhere","attachments","files","drawings"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Attaching Files to Records\n\nA part is more than its fields. There is the drawing, the customer's PO, the material cert, the photo of the fixture. Forge keeps those files on the record itself so the next person does not have to hunt through a shared drive or an inbox.\n\n### Where files live\n\nMost records that need paperwork have a **Files** area on their detail view: parts (the Files section), quotes and sales orders (customer documents), employees (their documents tab), and others. Open the record and look for the upload box; the exact spot varies by page, but the box itself is always the same.\n\n### Adding a file\n\nThe upload box says **Drag files here or click to browse**. Either drag files from your desktop onto it or click it and pick them. You can add several at once. Each file shows a progress bar while it uploads, then joins the list below. Large files are sent in pieces so a slow connection does not have to start over if it hiccups.\n\nThe box tells you the size limit and, where the page only takes certain kinds of files, which types are accepted. Common ones are PDF, images (JPG, PNG), Word and Excel files, and for parts the CAD and mesh formats such as STEP, STL and DXF. Something outside the list is refused with a message rather than uploaded and ignored.\n\n### Viewing files\n\nClick a file in the list to open it. PDFs open in a viewer inside Forge, images open in a lightbox you can page through, and 3-D models open in a viewer you can rotate. Anything else downloads. Files are also where job cover photos come from: on a job you can pick any attached image as the card's cover.\n\n### Keeping it tidy\n\n- Attach the file to the record it is *about*: the drawing on the part, the PO on the sales order, the cert on the lot or receipt.\n- Name files so the list reads well without opening them: `A-1042 Rev C drawing.pdf` beats `scan0001.pdf`.\n- When a drawing changes revision, upload the new one rather than editing the old; the record keeps its history.\n- Uploads count against the size limit your admin has set (Admin > Settings > Max Upload Size). If a scan is too big, lower its resolution before uploading.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Importing and Exporting Data",
            Slug = "everywhere-import-export",
            Summary = "Load parts and customers from a spreadsheet, and get data back out as CSV or Excel from any list or the CPA exports page",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 6,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 5,
            AppRoutes = """["/parts/import","/customers/import","/accounting/exports","/parts","/customers"]""",
            Tags = """["everywhere","import","export","csv","excel","spreadsheet"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Importing and Exporting Data\n\nForge does not expect you to key in a thousand parts by hand. If you have them in a spreadsheet, you can bring them in from a CSV file; and anything you can see in a list you can take back out to a spreadsheet.\n\n### Importing parts and customers\n\nThe **Import** button at the top of the Parts and Customers lists opens a two-step page.\n\n**1. Source.** Either click **Upload CSV** and pick a file, or paste rows straight from a spreadsheet into the text box (comma- or tab-separated both work) and click **Parse**. The first row must be a header row. The page lists the columns it understands, and it recognizes common variations, so `Part Name`, `name` and `Description` all land where you expect.\n\n- **Parts**: name (required), description, inventory class (raw, component, subassembly, finished good, consumable, tool), procurement (make, buy, subcontract, phantom) and, optionally, a part number. Anything it cannot read for class or procurement defaults to Component and Buy. Part numbers are assigned automatically unless you supply them.\n- **Customers**: name (required), company, email, phone, notes.\n\nIf a row cannot be read at all, the problem is listed under the box and nothing is imported until you fix it or remove the row.\n\n**2. Preview & commit.** Click **Preview** and Forge checks every row without saving anything. Each row gets a status: *Will create*, *Invalid*, *Duplicate in file*, or *Already exists* (it matches a record you already have). Two chips at the top summarize how many will be created and how many skipped. When it looks right, click **Commit**. Only the *Will create* rows are saved; duplicates and invalid rows are left alone, and the final message tells you how many of the total actually went in.\n\nBecause preview and commit are separate, it is safe to try an import and back out. **Reset** clears the page so you can start over with a fixed file.\n\n### Exporting from any list\n\nEvery list has **Export CSV** and **Export Excel** buttons at the top-right corner of the table. The file contains exactly what you are looking at — your filters, your sort, your visible columns. Narrow the list first and you have a report without writing one.\n\n### CPA exports\n\nUnder Accounting, the **CPA Exports** page produces the files your accountant asks for: **Trial Balance CSV**, **GL Detail CSV** and **Journal Summary CSV**. Pick the date range (there are presets for this month and the last 30 days) and click the file you want. These are one-way downloads; nothing you do in a spreadsheet afterwards flows back into Forge.\n\n### A few cautions\n\n- Import creates new records; it does not update existing ones. Rows that match an existing record are skipped, not merged.\n- Keep the header row. Without it the page cannot tell which column is which.\n- Open your CSV in a text editor if a preview looks odd — stray commas inside a description are the usual culprit; put that value in quotes.","sections":[]}"""
        });

