        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "When Someone Else Changed the Record",
            Slug = "everywhere-record-changed",
            Summary = "What the Record Changed and Sync Conflict messages mean, and how to keep your work without overwriting a coworker's",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 4,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 7,
            AppRoutes = """["/sales-orders","/purchase-orders","/invoices","/quotes","/kanban"]""",
            Tags = """["everywhere","conflicts","saving","offline"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## When Someone Else Changed the Record\n\nTwo people can have the same sales order open at once. If you both save, whose change wins? In Forge the answer is: the first one, and the second person is told. This is what stops a coworker's edit from being quietly wiped out by yours.\n\n### The Record Changed message\n\nYou open a record, make an edit, click Save — and instead of saving, a small dialog says **Record Changed: This record was changed by another user. Reload and try again?** Your save was refused because the copy on your screen is older than the one in Forge.\n\nYou have two choices:\n\n- **Reload** — fetches the current version of the record. Your unsaved edits on that record are discarded, so note what you changed before you click it. Then make your edit again on the fresh copy and save.\n- **Cancel** — keeps your screen exactly as it is, edits and all. Nothing is saved. Use this if you want to copy your changes somewhere first.\n\nEither way, the other person's change is safe. That is the point.\n\n### Why it happens\n\nEvery time a record is saved it gets a new version stamp. When you save, Forge checks that the stamp on your copy matches the one it has. If a coworker saved in between, the stamps differ and you see the message. It applies to the records where a silent overwrite would hurt most: jobs, quotes, sales orders, purchase orders, invoices, payments and shipments.\n\n### The Sync Conflict message\n\nIf you were working offline (the connection banner was showing), your changes wait in a queue and are sent when you are back online. If one of them runs into a newer version on the server, you get a **Sync Conflict** dialog instead. It shows the action that was waiting, what the server says about it, and your change. Pick **Keep Server** to drop your queued change and accept what is in Forge now, or **Keep Mine** to send your change anyway and overwrite. **Cancel** leaves the item in the queue for you to decide later.\n\n### Habits that avoid conflicts\n\n- Save small edits promptly rather than leaving a record open all afternoon.\n- If you know two people work the same orders, agree on who owns which stage.\n- After a Reload, re-read the record before re-applying your change — the other person may have already done what you were about to do.","sections":[]}"""
        });

        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Why You See What You See",
            Slug = "everywhere-permissions",
            Summary = "How roles decide which menus, pages and buttons appear for you, what Access denied means, and how your landing screen is chosen",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 5,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 8,
            AppRoutes = """["/dashboard","/account/customization","/admin/users"]""",
            Tags = """["everywhere","roles","permissions","landing","navigation"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Why You See What You See\n\nTwo people can sign in to the same Forge and see different menus. That is not a glitch. What you see is shaped by the roles your administrator has given you.\n\n### Hidden, not greyed out\n\nForge's rule is simple: if you are not allowed to do something, the button or menu for it is not shown at all. There are no greyed-out buttons to wonder about. A shop-floor user does not see Accounting in the menu; an office user without the Manager role does not see the Archive button on a job. If a coworker has a button you do not, the difference is almost always a role.\n\nThe same rule applies to whole pages. The sidebar only lists the areas your roles allow, and inside a page, sections and actions that are outside your roles are left out.\n\n### Two locks on every door\n\nHiding a button is a courtesy; it is not the security. Every action is also checked by the server when it happens. If something slips through — an old link, a bookmark to a page you used to have — you get a plain **Access denied. You do not have permission for this action.** message and nothing changes. The message deliberately does not say what the record was or who owns it.\n\nIf you need to do something you cannot see, ask your administrator to add the role. You cannot grant it to yourself.\n\n### Features that are switched off\n\nThere is a second reason a menu item can be missing that has nothing to do with you: the feature is turned off for your installation. Forge is made of capabilities that an administrator switches on and off under Admin > Capabilities. A page for a feature that is off is hidden for everybody, whatever their roles. The training catalog behaves the same way, so a course about a feature you do not have will not show up either.\n\n### Your landing screen\n\nWhen you sign in, Forge picks the page most useful to you. If you have exactly one role, you land on that role's home: production workers and production managers on the Job Board, planners on Planning, engineers on Parts, procurement on Purchasing, controllers on Accounting, office managers on Customers, compliance officers on Compliance and project managers on the Backlog. Anyone with broader or multiple roles lands on the Dashboard.\n\nYou can override this. Under **Account > Customization**, the **Landing screen** setting lets you choose a specific page — Dashboard, Job Board, Backlog, Parts, Customers, Sales Orders, Purchasing, Inventory, Scheduling, Reports or Accounting — or leave it on *Automatic (based on your role)*. If the page you chose is switched off for your installation, you land on the Dashboard instead.\n\n### Links between records\n\nAcross Forge, references to other records — the customer on an order, the part on a job, the order behind an invoice — are links. Click one to jump to that record. On a desktop, hovering over a link shows a small preview card with the record's basics and shortcuts to related records, so you can check a detail without leaving the page. If you would rather not have the popups, turn off **Desktop preview popups** under Account > Customization. The preview only ever shows information your roles already allow you to see.","sections":[]}"""
        });

